  "evidence_classes": {
    "contact_physics": "PhysX rigid-body simulation. The RG2 holds a brick through friction and a force-limited joint drive; no attachment joint is created at grasp. Brick contact forces and motion are solver outputs, not sensor readings.",
    "simulated_connection": "Stud insertion is not simulated. The brick is pressed against the support; when gap, tilt, yaw, position and force thresholds pass, the assembly checker snaps it to the nearest stud position and adds a weld constraint, closing the recorded pre-connection gap. Breakage checking is off.",
    "ground_truth_corrections": "The controller reads exact simulated brick and support poses: at the grasp, when planning placement from the held brick's true pose, and to correct the held brick's XY before descent. Inputs can disable the last two (C09, C10, C13), but source and support poses stay ground truth in every case. Hardware has no such measurement.",
    "other_assumptions": "Fixed lab scene and rigid base at the USD pose; kinematic baseplate; RMPflow commands applied to simulated joint drives with no controller latency, backlash or calibration error; invisible tabletop collider; simulated mass and friction are not measured from real bricks."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "Simulation only. These results do not establish physical robot performance.",
    "Each case ran once in a fixed scene: this is not a success rate.",
    "Injected errors are commanded offsets, not measured real-world error models.",
    "Thresholds of 2 mm, 5 degrees and 1 N are simulator settings, not brick tolerances measured on hardware.",
    "Simulated force values are not calibrated to physical force."
  ],
